.TH HOWDY 1 "@HOWDY_MAN_DATE@" "Howdy Next @PROJECT_VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
howdy \- facial authentication management for Linux
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B howdy
[\fIoptions\fR] \fIcommand\fR [\fIarguments\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B howdy
manages enrolled face models, camera testing, and configuration for Howdy Next.
PAM authentication is provided by
.BR pam_howdy (8).
.PP
Model commands target the invoking user automatically; pass
.B \-U
/
.B \-\-user
to target another account.
.SH COMMANDS
@HOWDY_COMMAND_REFERENCE@
.SH OPTIONS
@HOWDY_OPTION_REFERENCE@
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
sudo howdy config
sudo howdy add
sudo howdy test
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I @HOWDY_CONFIG_PATH_ROFF@
Configuration file.
.TP
.I @HOWDY_MODELS_DIR_ROFF@
OpenCV ONNX model directory.
.TP
.I @HOWDY_USER_MODELS_DIR_ROFF@
Enrolled user face models.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
Returns 0 on success and nonzero on error.
.SH "SECURITY NOTES"
Howdy's weak point is the camera. A plain RGB webcam can be fooled
by a photo or a face shown on a screen.
.B Use an IR camera:
photos and LCD displays do not reproduce IR light, which blocks those spoofs.
Keep config and model paths root-controlled.
.SH AUTHOR
Originally written by boltgolt. Howdy Next is maintained by nathawat on GitHub.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR howdy.ini (5),
.BR pam_howdy (8),
.BR pam (8),
.BR sudo (8)
